{
  "image_name": "33be4d56b8e29143c5cb855c2d80f12e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how polymer coatings on lithium metal can react with the metal and shape the interphase that forms at the surface. Overall, it shows that a coating can convert into a purple reaction layer at the Li interface, creating a chemically modified region rather than remaining inert. The central idea is that specific functional groups in the polymer are lithiated or otherwise transformed on contact with lithium.\n\nSeveral example reactions are illustrated: cyclic ether units open to form lithium alkoxides, sulfonyl fluoride becomes a lithium sulfonyl species with LiF formation, and UPy-like motifs appear to be deprotonated/lithiated. Carboxylic acids and alcohols likewise convert to lithium carboxylates and alkoxides, while fluoroalkyl groups can eliminate LiF. These examples indicate a range of polymer chemistries that can produce ionically active or protective interfacial species.\n\nA second concept is selectivity of interphase formation. One schematic contrasts an undesired solvent-derived SEI with a preferred salt-derived SEI, where the coating blocks solvent access but still allows ions to reach the lithium surface. The bottom panels then suggest design rules for coating side chains: solvent-phobic groups such as fluoroether or alkyl segments, and salt-philic groups such as pyrrolidinium-TFSI, Li-sulfonate, and urea, to promote favorable ion transport while suppressing solvent decomposition.",
